+# Visual repair
+
+Use for an existing image with a visible physical, spatial, identity, copy, or
+composition problem. Inspect the pixels first; a previous prompt is not proof.
+Inspect a relevant crop when the failure is too small to read at normal scale.
+
+Before the call, state a compact repair contract (or supply these fields through
+the project's canonical compiler):
+
+- Current visible issue: the object, body part, surface, gaze, contact, or text
+  that is wrong.
+- Desired visible reading: what a cold viewer must understand.
+- Edit boundary: exactly what can change.
+- Physical state: which object side or state faces the viewer and what cannot
+  appear there (for example rear phone cameras cannot share the front screen).
+- Gaze and contact: where eyes, hands, tools, and shadows must land.
+- Preserve: locked copy, identity, composition, style, and staging.
+- Acceptance: visible conditions checked on the returned pixels.
+
+When correct physical staging hides a story detail, do not put that detail on an
+impossible surface. Use permitted pose/context cues or a permitted camera change.
+Locks take precedence over this suggestion. Do not substitute a new angle,
+physical state, or premise without authorization when it changes a locked choice.
+
+Follow the project's retry budget. For standalone repairs, after two failures
+of the same physical premise, stop that premise. A new staging is an option only
+within existing creative authority; otherwise report what failed and request the
+specific decision needed. Inspect every return before claiming a repair worked.
+A passing pixel check does not grant project or creator approval.
